{"ratings":[{"id":"F1","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A plausible conflict resolution can erase the PR's entire change while the factory reports success and pushes it."},{"id":"F2","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A reviewer can approve an incomplete resolution because the review omits PR changes dropped from a conflicted file."},{"id":"F3","consequence":"compat","trigger":"plausible","reason":"After a rollback and re-upgrade, differing round-numbering schemes can cause a uniqueness collision that prevents further reviews of the PR."},{"id":"F4","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Delivery bookkeeping after a crash can invalidate a valid approval and mislabel another blocked landing attempt."},{"id":"F5","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A stale cached snapshot can permanently skip an eligible conflict trigger and leave the run unresolved."},{"id":"F6","consequence":"resume","trigger":"unusual","reason":"A restart can repeat a recorded push and undo an operator's branch reset made during the crash window."},{"id":"F7","consequence":"test","trigger":"plausible","reason":"The current push is pinned correctly, but the tests do not detect removal of the explicit commit argument."},{"id":"F8","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"normal","reason":"An unread delivered-conflict event can be omitted from the digest's review queue, causing the operator to miss a needed approval."}]}